	"comments": [
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " A relational chain keeps a paren pair around its `<` operand whenever the region",
			"start": 20,
			"end": 103
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " between the `<` and the `>` would parse as a type-argument list — and the token past",
			"start": 105,
			"end": 192
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " the `>` is not the axis. Every follower here is an expression head, which is exactly",
			"start": 194,
			"end": 281
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " what makes the input a comparison chain; bare, the first break the printer takes at",
			"start": 283,
			"end": 369
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " the `>` re-lexes every one of them for tsv's own parse, which commits a type-argument",
			"start": 371,
			"end": 459
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " list past a line terminator ahead of any expression (tsc keeps the chain at `+` / `-`).",
			"start": 461,
			"end": 551
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " A string, a numeric and a bare-identifier `<` operand ask the same question.",
			"start": 554,
			"end": 633
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " So does every follower shape: a literal, a prefix operator, an array, an object and",
			"start": 713,
			"end": 799
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " a `typeof` operand.",
			"start": 801,
			"end": 823
		},
		{
			"type": "Line",
			"value": " An indexed operand is as much a type as a reference one, so it takes the pair too.",
			"start": 1076,
			"end": 1161
		}
	],
